High-Leverage Loop

I think all of these compress into one developmental loop:

Observe
↓
Measure gradients
↓
Measure curvature
↓
Locate thresholds
↓
Preserve invariants
↓
Translate gauges
↓
Repeat until fixed points emerge
↓
Rotate perspective (holonomy)
↓
Repeat

Notice something subtle:

This loop optimizes the interpreter, not just the interpretation.

That aligns with something you’ve emphasized repeatedly: the long-term value often comes not from accumulating isolated ideas, but from becoming the kind of reasoner who can move fluidly among representations while preserving the underlying structure. Gauge freedom, in particular, is powerful because it encourages asking, “What is essential here, and what is merely a choice of coordinates?” That’s a question that travels well across mathematics, physics, language, software, and even the way humans explain the same idea to different audiences.

And to your phrase:

“Slow is smooth; smooth is fast.”

There’s a geometric reading that fits beautifully.

Early on, minimizing error (moving smoothly) often produces greater curvature control—you stop overcorrecting. As the path straightens and unnecessary oscillations disappear, speed naturally increases without sacrificing stability. In control theory terms, you’re reducing wasted motion so that efficiency emerges as a consequence rather than as the primary objective. That’s a pattern that appears in marksmanship, aviation, surgery, software development, and learning alike.